Units upgrade in their own cities for gold, as in Civilization III. The engine had the upgrade chains and obsolescence but no way to upgrade a unit, so an obsolete garrison stayed obsolete for the rest of the game, and the AI's gold sat idle once its cities built Wealth. MapUnit.Upgrade (MapUnit_Upgrade.cs) turns a unit standing in one of its owner's cities into the furthest unit along its civ's upgrade chain that the city can build now (UnitPrototype.UpgradeTargetIn: the tech, a coastal city for ships, the strategic resources connected), for the shield difference (at least 1) times Rules.UpgradeCostPerShield gold, 3 by default since Conquests' upgrade cost is not imported. The unit keeps its id, name if renamed, experience, hit points (up to the new maximum), fortification and position, and uses up its moves for the turn; it is refused without the gold. The AI (PlayerAI.MaybeUpgradeUnits, after it picks a government) spends the gold above a reserve of a turn's upkeep (at least 50) on upgrading its combat units in its cities, the largest gain in defence first, then attack, in unit order, so games replay the same; units that do not fight are left alone, and a weak economy upgrades nothing. Because the budget keeps the treasury near even, the stock alone paid for few upgrades (12 and 32 units upgraded by T540 in two Standard games), so while a city's best defender could upgrade to a stronger one, AdjustSliders keeps one tenth of the commerce from science for it (44 and 114). City.GetAccessibleResources and UnitPrototype.GetUpgradeChain are made public for the bridge, which explains a refused upgrade.

